// What the mover cog keeps between executions: the tick's state, the base it
// composes over, and the overlays playing on it.
//
// The mover is the writer of every field here, which is why the module sits in
// `cogs/` beside it. What it holds of other components is held whole: the
// tick's state is `motion/tick_state.clk`'s, an overlay row is
// `clips/player.clk`'s, and the two schedule facts it remembers are
// `cogs/schedule.clk`'s. Nothing is restated.
//
// Time is `SyncTime` and `Duration` throughout, and the totals are all-run
// counts a reader compares between executions rather than rates.

#![generate(cpp)]
#![cpp(namespace=brenn_reachy::cogs)]

use clips::player;
use cogs::schedule;
use motion::targets as target_vocab;
use motion::tick_state;

// The base an overlay composes over, while the mover is the one sampling it.
//
// Ordinarily the tick owns the base: the cog commands a move and the tick
// shapes it. A composed setpoint is commanded per period instead, which
// abandons any move the tick is running -- so for as long as an overlay window
// is open the cog carries the base itself, and this is where it lives. `owned`
// is the handover: false while the tick has the base, true from the period an
// overlay first composes until the last window closes.
schema BaseSnap
{
    uuid: 345a620e-07b0-4b1f-baf3-a6d896b98c9b;
    fields
    {
        // Whether the cog is sampling the base. Every field below means nothing
        // when this is false.
        #0 owned: Bool;

        // Where the base stands: the configuration an overlay is composed over
        // this period. Not the last thing commanded -- that is the composed
        // setpoint, which is the base plus whatever was playing.
        #1 targets: target_vocab::Targets;

        // The base move in flight, or that none is. Taken over from the tick on
        // the period the handover happens, and planned here for a step that
        // arrives while the cog holds the base.
        #2 path: tick_state::TrajectorySeed;

        // How far into that move, on its own clock. One period per sample, the
        // same arithmetic the tick does.
        #3 elapsed: Duration;
    }
}

// What the decision tick carries between samples: the tick's own state, and
// what the cog knows about the schedule it is following.
//
// The tick's state is the whole of `snap`, and everything beside it is this
// cog's: whether a state exists at all, and enough about the last schedule it
// acted on to tell a change from a repetition. Publish bookkeeping is absent:
// the cog reads sequence numbers and timestamps back from its own view of the
// channel it publishes on.
schema MoverState
{
    uuid: f4cb808c-2637-48ba-a16d-1041319208dd;
    fields
    {
        // Whether a motion state exists: the session is engaged and a solve
        // from a measured pose produced one. Until it does, `snap` is whatever
        // a default-constructed slot holds and describes no machine.
        #0 armed: Bool;

        // The tick's state.
        #1 snap: tick_state::MotionSnap;

        // The `epoch` of the last schedule acted on. A schedule arriving with
        // this number again asks for nothing new, whatever its steps say.
        #2 schedule_epoch_seen: UInt32;

        // The kind of the last step dispatched as a move.
        #3 desired_kind: schedule::StepKind;

        // The posture of that step. Together with `desired_kind` this is what a
        // fresh schedule is compared against to decide whether the machine has
        // somewhere new to be.
        #4 desired_posture: schedule::Posture;

        // How many goal datagrams have been published, all run.
        #5 goals_published: UInt64;

        // How many reports the tick raised, all run.
        #6 faults_raised: UInt64;

        // How many samples have been seen, all run.
        #7 samples_seen: UInt64;

        // How many messages on the sample channel failed validation, all run.
        // Distinct from a sample that carried no reading: those bytes describe
        // no sample at all, so they name no instant and no positions and no
        // tick ran on them.
        #8 refused_samples: UInt64;

        // How many reports were raised on an execution that had already
        // reported one, all run. What a reader compares against the reports in
        // the log to know whether it is reading all of them.
        #9 reports_dropped: UInt64;

        // How many times the tick state in this slot could not be read back as
        // a machine, or would not go back into it, all run. Either way the cog
        // is left unarmed and arms again off the next sample, so a rise says an
        // engagement ended without anything having asked for it.
        #10 refused_state: UInt64;

        // How many times an epoch this cog had not answered yet was answered by
        // a posture step, all run. An epoch stands until a posture step covers
        // an instant, and one execution sees only the latest schedule, so two
        // bumps published inside one gap are one answer here: the total counts
        // epoch changes this cog observed, not epochs a session published, and
        // subtracting it from a publish count says nothing. Whether the epoch
        // published now is outstanding is `schedule_epoch_seen` against it.
        #11 epochs_answered: UInt64;

        // The base, while this cog is the one sampling it.
        #12 base: BaseSnap;

        // The overlays playing, one row per window the schedule carries. The row
        // is the window's position in the schedule: a window keeps its row for
        // as long as it is open, which is what lets a player be picked up again
        // on the next execution rather than restarted every period.
        #13 players: FixedArray<player::ClipPlayerSnap, 4>;

        // Whether the configured clip library has been walked frame by frame.
        // The library is a configuration binding, immutable for the process's
        // life, so the walk is a fact about bytes that do not change: the first
        // execution that needs the library pays it and records it here, and
        // every later one takes it up without re-reading a frame.
        #14 library_walked: Bool;

        // Whether this cog has stopped playing the schedule named by
        // `latch_epoch`, because a composed setpoint carrying an overlay was
        // refused or because the clip library would not establish. While it
        // stands, no window is taken up and the base streams alone: the same
        // clip over the same base composes the same setpoint and the same
        // library refuses the same way, so a layer that took its windows up
        // again would repeat a refused ask once a period.
        #15 overlay_latch: Bool;

        // The `epoch` of the schedule the overlay layer last screened.
        // `overlay_latch` belongs to that schedule: a schedule arriving with a
        // different epoch is a fresh set of windows, so the latch is cleared
        // and the screen's refusals are counted again.
        #16 latch_epoch: UInt32;

        // How many overlay windows this cog would not play, all run. The
        // screen's refusals -- a window naming no motion, asking for a gain or
        // a speed that is not one, spanning no time -- counted once per
        // schedule rather than once per period, plus one for each composed
        // setpoint an overlay rode that the tick refused. An overlay is
        // presence, never safety: every one of these costs the run an overlay
        // and nothing else.
        #17 overlays_refused: UInt64;

        // How many overlay rows would not read back as a player of the motion
        // their window names, all run. The window plays on from a fresh join at
        // its own offset, which is what a window opening does anyway.
        #18 players_refused: UInt64;

        // How many base plans this cog could not make or could not read back,
        // all run: a plan the machine refused -- a target outside the envelope,
        // an antenna direction no count reaches, a move nothing can shape --
        // and a base record in this slot that was not one. Both leave the base
        // standing where it stands, which is why they share a total.
        #19 refused_base: UInt64;

        // How many base plans the motion library had to adjust for a reason
        // that is not routine, all run: a clock lengthened to carry its own
        // span inside the per-tick step bounds, or an antenna pair the pass
        // could not part to the separation that keeps their tips clear. A
        // duration is configuration, sized for the spans an ordinary command
        // covers; where the machine physically stands is not, so a plan over a
        // span the configured clock cannot carry is lengthened rather than
        // refused. Zero on a healthy machine is the invariant this field exists
        // to make legible; the de-phasing every posture move asks for is
        // routine and is counted next door, in `base_dephased`.
        #20 base_stretched: UInt64;

        // How many base plans were adjusted only to de-phase the antenna pair,
        // all run: the configured separation reached by lengthening the later
        // side's clock, on clocks that each carried their own span. Two
        // antennas sweeping between the stow and the working posture arrive
        // mirrored, which is where their tips meet, so this is expected nonzero
        // on any machine that stands up or folds -- it is the evidence the
        // pair-phase guard is working, not a fault signal.
        #21 base_dephased: UInt64;
    }
}
